  "TITLE": "Why Friction Drops<br>When Things Speed Up",
  "LEAD": "At low speed or heavy load, two metal surfaces touch peak to peak and friction is high; as speed rises, oil is dragged into the gap until a film lifts the surfaces apart &mdash; the Stribeck transition, where wear nearly vanishes. Your knee runs the same curve: standing up from a chair squeezes the cartilage thin, while steady walking pumps fluid back in.",
